diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage on it_lab3. The host’s network stack and path look healthy end-to-end:
- Interface state: eth0 is UP/LOWER_UP with link detected (ethtool shows link yes, 10G Full). IP is 192.168.1.5/24 with a valid default route via 192.168.1.1.
- L2 evidence: ARP table on it_lab3 has entries for 192.168.1.1, 1.2, 1.3 with MACs, indicating normal neighbor resolution.
- L3 reachability: From it_lab3 I can ping the local gateway 192.168.1.1 and peers 192.168.1.2/.3 with 0% loss and sub-ms RTTs. I can also reach other subnets (e.g., office_pc 192.168.3.2 and internet LAN host 128.168.0.2) with 0% loss, confirming routing through the campus core is working.

Mechanism: If a switch port or NIC had “died,” we would see eth0 down/no link and ARP failures. Instead, both L1/L2 and L3 are functioning, and traffic counters increment when tested.

Localization: it_lab3 eth0 on the IT LAN (via switch0) and router0’s IT gateway path are all operational now.

Root cause: The reported isolation is not present now; likely a transient host or link flap that self-cleared, or a user/application-layer issue unrelated to network reachability.

Corrective fix: No change required. Recommend monitoring for recurrences; if it reappears, check eth0 dmesg for link flaps, reseat/replace patch cable, and review switch0’s IT-facing port error counters. === END ===